Document 1: API Client & Mocking Layer
Summary

This document describes the API client and mocking layer used in FutureFlow to support frontend development and testing. This layer simulates backend interactions and allows features to function without a fully implemented backend.

Rationale

To avoid blocking frontend development, an API abstraction layer is used alongside mocked data. This enables the application to behave as if it is communicating with a real backend while still under development.

Design Overview
API Client

The API client serves as the interface between the frontend and backend. It is responsible for:

Sending and handling API requests
Standardizing data access across the application
Providing a consistent structure for future backend integration
Mocking Layer

The mocking layer simulates API responses using predefined data. It:

Returns mock career roles and skill data
Simulates missing skill identification
Provides fake progress tracking data for UI testing
Benefits
Enables parallel frontend and backend development
Provides consistent and predictable test data
Improves debugging and testing efficiency
Reduces integration risks later in development
Scope Control

This layer:

Does not implement real backend logic
Does not persist user data
Exists solely to simulate API interactions during development
Conclusion

The API client and mocking layer allow FutureFlow to build and test features independently of backend completion, ensuring efficient and scalable development.
